@@ -523,6 +523,22 @@ Created as part of AdvCiv-SAS improvements
 
 
 
+	<!-- custom: written with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks. -->
+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_SPANISH_LONJA_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]A lonja was a commercial exchange or trading hall where merchants gathered to conduct business. One of the most celebrated examples is the Lonja de la Seda, or Silk Exchange, of Valencia, constructed between 1482 and 1533 during a period of exceptional commercial prosperity in the city.[PARAGRAPH:1]Originally devoted to the silk trade, the complex became an important center of Mediterranean commerce. Its grand Sala de Contrataci�n, or Trading Hall, provided a monumental setting for mercantile transactions, while the complex also housed the Consulate of the Sea and Valencia's municipal banking institution, the Taula de Canvis. The wealth and scale of the Lonja reflected the importance attained by Iberian merchants during the 15th and 16th centuries.[PARAGRAPH:1]Architecturally, the Lonja de la Seda is a masterpiece of late Gothic secular architecture. Its Trading Hall is divided into three aisles beneath ribbed vaults supported by tall spiral columns, while richly ornamented windows, doors and gargoyles decorate the exterior. In 1996 it was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site as an exceptional surviving expression of Valencia's mercantile prosperity.[PARAGRAPH:1]Source: GPT-5.6 Thinking.</English>
+	</TEXT>
+
+
+
+	<!-- custom: based on https://apsaraauthority.gov.kh/2021/08/12/preah-khan-temple/ -->
+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_KHMER_HALL_OF_DANCERS_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]Preah Khan ("Sacred Sword") was a late-12th-century temple complex, city, and center of Buddhist learning. In 1191, ten years after ascending the throne, Jayavarman VII dedicated its central image to his father Dharanindra, represented as Lokesvara, the bodhisattva of compassion.[PARAGRAPH:1]The foundation stele records the temple's Sanskrit name as Jayacri. During the Middle Period, a stupa replaced the image of Lokesvara in the central sanctuary, allowing the sanctuary to represent multiple forms of Buddhism.[PARAGRAPH:1]The Hall of Dancers is named for the apsara carvings decorating its walls. Other notable features include a pediment of Vishnu reclining on the serpent Ananta, Garudas on the fourth enclosure wall, a unique two-storey structure with round columns, and giant silk-cotton trees growing among the ruins.[PARAGRAPH:1]Preah Khan was more than a temple: its large resident population was organized by function, and the complex supported Buddhist teachers and their disciples.[PARAGRAPH:1]Source: https://apsaraauthority.gov.kh/2021/08/12/preah-khan-temple/</English>
